WHAT THAT LICENSES, per reading:

  (A) shape helicity -- -1 at n=+1, +1 at n=-1, pair sum 0.
      INTEGER-VALUED. A 0.029% common-mode asymmetry cannot move a quantity that
      changes only in discrete jumps. The exact flip is ROBUST to the drift, and
      would remain so at a hundred times the asymmetry.

  (B) core-circuit winding W -- -1.19 at n=+1, +1.87 at n=-1, pair sum +0.68.
      CONTINUOUS, so in principle drift-sensitive. But the residual is 44.4% of
      the typical magnitude (1.53), which exceeds the measured branch asymmetry
      by a factor of ~1,530. B'S PAIR-SUM FAILURE IS THEREFORE A REAL FEATURE OF
      THE CONFIGURATION, NOT A NUMERICAL ARTIFACT -- it cannot be explained away
      by the drift, and equally it cannot be rescued by tightening the integrator.

CONSEQUENCE, COMMITTED NOW: if the run completes with these values holding, then
reading (A) is the surviving one and reading (B) is falsified on its own
pair-sum, independently of whatever sign the completed run reports. If instead
(A)'s flip fails to hold at completion, that is a genuine surprise and neither
reading survives -- the failure would then be in the construction, not the
arithmetic.

WHAT IS NOT LICENSED: any statement about energy, action, or stability from this
run; any comparison to a run with a different integrator setting; and any claim
that (B) would pass with more resolution, since its residual is three orders
above the noise that resolution controls.

--- pre-registration re-checked at t = 6.75/8 (2026-07-28) ---
Shared frames 22 -> 24. Worst relative branch asymmetry UNCHANGED at 0.0294%,
and the two frames added since the note (t = 6.50, 6.75) differ by only -0.004
and -0.003 pp -- smaller than the mid-run frames, not larger. Absolute drift
meanwhile climbed 76.3% -> 78.8%.

That is the common-mode signature the acceptance note assumed, now tested rather
than asserted: the drift is a property of the integrator and is identical between
the two winding signs, so it cancels in the branch comparison while remaining
fatal to any absolute energetic claim. The criterion stands as written, and
reading (A)'s integer flip remains robust by three orders against this asymmetry.

================================================================================
RESTORED NOTE + AN ADMISSION ABOUT THIS FILE (written 2026-07-28 22:10, after the
run exited)
================================================================================

WHAT HAPPENED. The original "PRE-REGISTERED ACCEPTANCE NOTE", appended to this log
at t = 6.25/8, IS GONE FROM THIS FILE. I appended it with >> while the run still
held the file open through a > redirection. The run's own write offset was behind
my append, so its subsequent frames overwrote my text. The later re-check note
survived only by the timing of the next flush. This file is not tracked in git, so
the original is unrecoverable.

WHY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN A LOST PARAGRAPH. The note's entire value was that it
fixed the acceptance criterion BEFORE the answer was visible. A pre-registration
that cannot be shown to predate its result is worth very little, and destroying
the primary record of one is a self-inflicted wound to exactly the discipline it
was serving.

WHAT THE ATTESTATION NOW RESTS ON, stated so a reader can weigh it:
  * scripts/toroidal_fork_verdict.py carries the criterion verbatim in its
    docstring and was written at 21:40:3x. The run reached t = 8.00 at ~22:07 and
    the process exited 22:08 -- a 27-minute margin, on a file nobody edited after.
  * The surviving re-check note above refers back to "the acceptance note" and
    "the criterion stands as written", corroborating that the original existed and
    said what is restored below.
  * This is a WEAKER attestation than an untouched original. It is stated as such.

THE CRITERION, restored verbatim in substance:

  * the branch E-drift asymmetry is common-mode, measured at 0.029% worst case
    over the shared frames -- the drift belongs to the integrator, not to the
    winding sign, so it cancels in a branch comparison;
  * (A) shape helicity is INTEGER-VALUED. A 0.029% common-mode asymmetry cannot
    move an integer, so an exact pair sum of zero is ROBUST;
  * (B) core-circuit winding is CONTINUOUS, hence drift-sensitive in principle.
    Its pair-sum residual must be compared against the asymmetry: a residual far
    above 0.029% of the typical magnitude is a real feature of the configuration,
    not numerical noise, and equally cannot be rescued by a finer integrator;
  * NO ABSOLUTE ENERGETIC QUANTITY may be quoted from this run at any point.

  COMMITTED CONSEQUENCE: if (A) flips exactly and (B) fails its pair sum, then (A)
  survives and (B) is falsified on its own pair sum. If (A)'s flip fails, neither
  survives and the fault is in the construction rather than the arithmetic.

THE RESULT, for the record: both branches completed with 29 frames (t = 1.00 ->
8.00). (A) = -1 and +1, pair sum 0, exact flip. (B) = -1.190 and +1.870, pair sum
+0.680 = 44.4% of the typical magnitude, exceeding the 0.0294% asymmetry by 1512x.
(A) SURVIVES; (B) FALSIFIED ON ITS OWN PAIR SUM -- the pre-committed outcome.

RULE FOR NEXT TIME: never append to a log a running process holds open. Write
notes to a SEPARATE file (<run>.notes.md) and reconcile afterwards.
